//? Title: Multiplicity and nuisance-adjusted inference
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Check sensitivity to the Storey threshold

Storey's method estimates the null fraction from the large p-values.
Changing the threshold changes which values enter that estimate. The
table compares two thresholds with the Benjamini–Hochberg baseline.
Each row keeps the hypotheses in their original order.

```{cpp, id=storey-sensitivity, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/discovery_bounds.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const std::vector<double> p{.002, .015, .016, .055, .19, .7, .9};
    const auto fits = datamunge::stats::storey_qvalues(p, {0, .25, .5});
    std::println("| Threshold | Estimated null fraction | q for p = .002 | q for p = .016 |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& row : fits)
        std::println("| {:.2f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", row.threshold,
                     row.null_fraction, row.q_values[0], row.q_values[2]);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Threshold | Estimated null fraction | q for p = .002 | q for p = .016 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.00 | 1.000 | 0.0140 | 0.0373 |
| 0.25 | 0.571 | 0.0080 | 0.0213 |
| 0.50 | 0.857 | 0.0120 | 0.0320 |
// result_end

> Bound discoveries in a selected subset

One closed Simes analysis allows several overlapping subset queries at the
same confidence level. Under the Simes inequality the lower bounds hold
simultaneously, including for subsets chosen after looking at the p-values.
The confidence level applies to the whole family of queries.

```{cpp, id=simes-sets, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/discovery_bounds.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const datamunge::stats::SimesDiscoveryBounds family({.002, .015, .016, .055, .19, .7, .9}, .05);
    std::println("| Subset of hypothesis indices | 95% simultaneous lower bound |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| 0, 1, 2 | {} |", family.lower_bound({0, 1, 2}));
    std::println("| 1, 2, 3, 4 | {} |", family.lower_bound({1, 2, 3, 4}));
    std::println("| 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 | {} |", family.lower_bound({0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Subset of hypothesis indices | 95% simultaneous lower bound |
| --- | ---: |
| 0, 1, 2 | 2 |
| 1, 2, 3, 4 | 1 |
| 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 | 2 |
// result_end

> Permute residuals while retaining nuisance effects

The regression example tests a binary treatment after adjusting for a
continuous baseline measurement and an intercept. Freedman–Lane holds the
baseline fit fixed and permutes residuals from that fit. The partial F
statistic is recomputed on each reconstructed response. This calibration
assumes exchangeable errors and uses a reproducible Monte Carlo stream.
The nuisance fit generally makes the permutation calibration approximate.

```{cpp, id=freedman-lane, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/freedman_lane.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using Matrix = datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;
    const auto nuisance = Matrix::from_columns({
        std::vector<double>(10, 1),
        {2, 4, 2, 6, 4, 3, 8, 7, 5, 6}
    });
    const Matrix treatment(10, 1, {0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1});
    const std::vector<double> response{3.1, 9.0, 3.5, 10.2, 5.4, 4.2, 13.1, 10.3, 5.7, 10.7};
    datamunge::random::SplitMix64 rng(42);
    const auto fit = datamunge::stats::freedman_lane_test(response, nuisance, treatment, 999, rng);
    std::println("| Partial F | Numerator df | Denominator df | Permutation p-value |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| {:.3f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.3f} |",
                 fit.statistic, fit.parameter1, fit.parameter2, fit.p_value);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Partial F | Numerator df | Denominator df | Permutation p-value |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 41.238 | 1 | 7 | 0.002 |
// result_end

